WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.480
My name is Joe Holdcroft, and I lead one of our software engineering teams.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.560
I've been here almost two years.

3
00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.480
I worked with Guy and several of the early folks at Snyk before.

4
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.760
It's quite an exciting prospect to get to work with some of these folks again.

5
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.200
since then things have changed a lot.

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.080
We’re a fair bit bigger.

7
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.040
We've gone through a number of sort of different prototypes that we've built,

8
00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.840
a number of different products that we've put together.

9
00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.720
And we're just now sort of starting to get to the point where it feels like

10
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.840
there's a bit of traction.

11
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.840
We're sort of heading towards finding product market fit.

12
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.840
You know, as you'd sort of expect in a startup environment,

13
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.160
there's lots of ability to influence change and impact the product.

14
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.640
And I feel like that's especially the case here.

15
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.600
It's a really nascent space, we're building a tool for software

16
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.680
engineers, we are software engineers, All ideas are good ideas.

17
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.160
There's plenty of space to, like,

18
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.480
go and prototype something if you want to have a play.

19
00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.760
Like, you don't just have to go and show this to

20
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.440
somebody, you can actually, like, build it.

21
00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.960
And especially with the current AI tooling,

22
00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.800
you can really put something together in a short period of time

23
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.320
that you can then actually go and productionize.

24
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.320
So, a lot of the product development is actually like grassroots

25
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:06.800
development that comes from the software engineering team.

26
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.480
There was something that we announced a couple of weeks ago,

27
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.440
And that just came out of one of our developers

28
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.040
putting in a bit of time and putting something together

29
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.080
and just kind of people seeing that and getting their hands on it

30
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.480
made us realise how valuable it was.

31
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.120
So, invested a bit more time on it.

32
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.960
And, you know, in the space of under a week

33
00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:26.120
that went from a little hobby project that he’d thrown together, to something

34
00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:29.120
that we released and did a marketing campaign about.

35
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.400
One of the most important and notable parts

36
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.400
of the culture, I think is, the level of proficiency.

37
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.440
A lot of people here, like, have a bunch of experience in software development,

38
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:42.120
in like dev tooling companies, and/or like in the AI space.

39
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.560
So, I'm constantly learning things from the people around me.

40
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:49.360
And I think especially in this time where the nature of the role is changing

41
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.320
so rapidly, it sort of brings me a lot of comfort

42
00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.680
to feel like I'm working with, like, some of the best, best in class folks.

43
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.080
Yeah.

44
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:57.880
Like I say, one of the things that drew me to the company

45
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.400
and one of the things that keeps me here.
